NOTE: partial run - the full teardown suite was stopped by the 10-minute background limit after 55 passing cases and no failures; the GitLab/Forgejo cases were re-run alone in fm-teardown-gitlab-subset.test.log.
ok - teardown leaves opencode's busy plugin in the returned pool worktree as on main
ok - hidden untracked work is refused before return while a clean control returns
ok - content fallback distinguishes unavailable Git proof, containment, conflicts, and unlanded content
ok - status archival is opt-in and preserves the complete log
ok - config-absent teardown does not read or invoke record export
ok - unopted teardown ignores symlinked config when record export is absent
ok - teardown commits the record archive before deleting sources and fails closed when export cannot commit
ok - forced secondmate teardown exports task records from the child sub-state
ok - record export waits for endpoint quiescence and retry captures records changed after refusal
ok - forced secondmate retirement preserves two descendant levels and refuses linked child directories
ok - local missing-status retirement stays lock-free without creating an archive
ok - reused task IDs never overwrite archives and retention removes only old owned logs
ok - archive sources and directories reject symlinks; pruning never follows children links
ok - failed archive publication preserves presentation and watcher bookkeeping
ok - cursor retirement failure removes its archive so retry publishes one history
ok - archive publication and cold logs never re-wake or re-present
ok - retention failures warn without aborting unrelated teardown
ok - a missing teardown startup source refuses before cleanup
ok - an unreadable teardown startup source refuses before cleanup
ok - a missing adapter sibling refuses before cleanup
ok - a forced descendant with a missing adapter sibling refuses before cleanup
ok - a forced secondmate with a missing own adapter sibling refuses before child cleanup
ok - present required sources still reach the ordinary teardown refusal
ok - local-only worktree with HEAD on a fork remote is torn down and the home summary is refreshed
ok - teardown closes its own backlog item before reporting success
ok - teardown closes a landed Gerrit task with its change URL as a note and a GitHub task with --pr
ok - teardown closes a landed GitLab task with its merge request URL as a note and GitHub and Forgejo tasks with --pr
ok - teardown honors config/backlog-backend=manual and still finishes cleanly
ok - local-only worktree with truly unpushed work is refused (safety preserved)
ok - local-only worktree with work merged into local main is torn down (no regression)
ok - no-mistakes worktree with HEAD on origin is torn down (no regression)
ok - no-mistakes worktree with genuinely unlanded work is refused (safety preserved)
ok - local-only worktree with unpushed work is torn down under --force (escape hatch)
ok - fm-pr-check publishes the PR-ready line on a secondmate's parent channel once
ok - fm-pr-check withholds the PR-ready line until checks are green when config/ready-after-green is present
ok - fm-pr-check releases the PR-ready line on a checks-green report that carries surrounding text
ok - a secondmate home's teardown delivers the child's final line or refuses until it can
ok - teardown completes when an exact busy-state sidecar is already absent
ok - teardown retires a polytoken task's generated worktree wiring on pool return
ok - teardown never deletes a tracked .polytoken hook layer
ok - teardown never deletes a .polytoken hook layer firstmate did not generate
ok - teardown scopes .polytoken wiring removal to polytoken tasks
ok - herdr teardown removes pane-owned escalation dedupe state
ok - herdr flat teardown refuses before returning the isolated copy under lock contention and the retry completes cleanly
ok - herdr flat teardown never erases records when pane presence is unparseable
ok - herdr flat teardown preflight refuses before every destructive change
ok - herdr teardown refuses a foreign-owned per-account namespace (owner-shim arms need the non-Darwin stat branch; skipped on Darwin)
ok - forced secondmate teardown preflights every Herdr child before cleanup mutation
ok - forced secondmate teardown holds every descendant lifecycle and metadata lock
ok - forced secondmate teardown retains Herdr child identity until exact pane disappearance
ok - forced teardown retains a nested secondmate home and its grandchild's Herdr identity when the grandchild close is unconfirmed
ok - herdr projection teardown retires its journal only after confirming the exact recorded pane is gone
ok - herdr projection teardown retains every record when post-close presence is unknown
ok - herdr projection teardown surfaces failed focus restoration without turning confirmed cleanup into a hard failure
ok - teardown retires the task's own watcher markers and orphaned presentation journal, leaving other tasks' markers alone
